## What I did today
- Developed formal HRR chapters on:
  - Human Error Philosophy
  - Evidence-Based Decision Making
  - Separation of Facts, Inference, and Judgment
  - Layered Defense (Swiss Cheese Model)
- Decided not to discard earlier drafts; treat them as foundation and expand upward
- Strengthened doctrine: error taxonomy, evidence hierarchy, ladder of reasoning, independent barriers

## What I learned
- Reliability comes from systems that expect error, not from perfect individuals
- Confidence must never exceed the strength of evidence
- Facts, inference, and judgment must remain separable and reviewable
- Multiple independent barriers matter more than any single perfect check
- Earlier work should be refined and reorganized, not thrown away

## One small win
- Converted four core topics into durable, normative HRR doctrine

